  "theory": {
        "intro": "On the unit circle a point at angle $\\theta$ has coordinates $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta$. Its equation $x^2+y^2=1$ becomes the **Pythagorean identity** $\\cos^{2}\\theta+\\sin^{2}\\theta=1$. Dividing that identity by $\\cos^{2}\\theta$ or $\\sin^{2}\\theta$ gives two more.\n\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\\large}]\n\\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes, calc, arrows.meta}\n\\coordinate (O) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (P) at (1.147,1.638);\n\\coordinate (Q) at (1.147,0);\n\\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-2.6,0) -- (2.6,0) node[below right] {$x$};\n\\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.6) node[above left] {$y$};\n\\draw (O) circle (2);\n\\draw (O) -- (P) node[midway, above left] {$1$};\n\\draw (Q) -- (P);\n\\fill (P) circle (1.5pt);\n\\node[above right] at (P) {$P(\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)$};\n\\coordinate (p) at ($(Q)!0.25cm!(O)$);\n\\coordinate (q) at ($(Q)!0.25cm!(P)$);\n\\draw (p) -- ($(p)+(q)-(Q)$) -- (q);\n\\pic[draw, \"$\\theta$\", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle = Q--O--P};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]",
        "facts": [
          "$\\cos^{2}\\theta+\\sin^{2}\\theta=1$, which rearranges to $\\sin^{2}\\theta=1-\\cos^{2}\\theta$ and $\\cos^{2}\\theta=1-\\sin^{2}\\theta$.",
          "Dividing through by $\\cos^{2}\\theta$ gives $1+\\tan^{2}\\theta=\\sec^{2}\\theta$, for $\\cos\\theta\\neq 0$.",
          "Dividing through by $\\sin^{2}\\theta$ gives $1+\\cot^{2}\\theta=\\operatorname{cosec}^{2}\\theta$, for $\\sin\\theta\\neq 0$.",
          "Rearranged, these give the difference forms $\\sec^{2}\\theta-\\tan^{2}\\theta=1$ and $\\operatorname{cosec}^{2}\\theta-\\cot^{2}\\theta=1$.",
          "As a difference of squares, $1-\\sin^{2}\\theta=(1-\\sin\\theta)(1+\\sin\\theta)=\\cos^{2}\\theta$."
        ]
      },
